Z||000047||MinnGreenGT||12-26-2001||04:36 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by LT1 Chop Top:<BR><B>Minn,<P>How is the poster of the Chop Top coming along??  I'd really like to see!<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I'll try to post a JPG of it tonight when I get to that office (it's @ my 2nd job) And I'll see about sending one off to you if I can get it printed again (I printed one last week, but I accidentally moved a couple of layers incorrectly in photoshop)!  I'll also try to get a Skitime poster printed and mailed this week as well.||||reg||1
Z||000048||Denny||12-26-2001||06:07 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by MinnGreenGT:<BR><B>Denny & grgoyl86 -looks good.  What kind of machine are you guys using anyway?<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>First, the display looks great! I had grgoyl86 print a display that I'm using at shows, but I used a lower resolution to keep the size down to send to him in email, so the quality isn't so good.<P>The poster farther up in this thread is a high-res collage, 24"X30". The frame is an inexpensive poster frame available in most craft/hobby stores, Kmart or Walmart.  Grgoyl86 uses a large-format HP printer/plotter. He can give you the model and specs if you're interested.<P>The higher the resolution of the photos going in, the better the poster looks. Depending on how complicated a poster gets, we can compose and print for $15 to $25. The price would be lower if a person sends their own composition, just remember the GIGO rule applies (Garbage In Garbage Out). <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000049||MinnGreenGT||12-26-2001||08:28 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by LT1 Chop Top:<BR><B>Minn,<BR>How is the poster of the Chop Top coming along??  I'd really like to see!<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Sorry for the delay- here's where it's at right now.  Currently a 36MB layered PhotoShop file  (2 copies are 105MB flattened and saved to a printable EPS standard).<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/choptop_poster.jpg">  <P>Opinions?<P>Edit: The type is actually a really cool blue brushed metal.  The little white line on the bottom edge is the copyright line (Joel Patton in this case).  And the final size it's at (due to original resolution restrictions) is 9.5" tall x 24" wide.  I could go larger, but the resolution quality will start to fall apart.<p>[This message has been edited by MinnGreenGT (edited 12-26-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000050||grgoyl86||12-26-2001||08:58 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Denny:<BR><B>The poster farther up in this thread is a high-res collage, 24"X30". The frame is an inexpensive poster frame available in most craft/hobby stores, Kmart or Walmart.  Grgoyl86 uses a large-format HP printer/plotter. He can give you the model and specs if you're interested.<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>It is an HP Design 755CM, a wide-format inkjet. It's sitting here in my basement. I have a roll of High-Gloss paper. Man, is that stuff expensive!||||reg||1
